# Backend build context exclusions.
#
# The load-bearing rule here is `.env` below. The backend image is built with
# `COPY . .`, and backend/.env is gitignored but was NOT ignored here — so the
# image baked in JWT_SECRET, the TOTP issuer and the database password, and
# every `docker history` / registry push leaked them. Docker contexts ignore
# .gitignore, so this file has to repeat the rule. Keep it in sync with any new
# secret file.

# --- Secrets: never in an image layer ---
# The `**/` forms are not redundant: a bare `.env` pattern only matches at the
# context root, so a dotenv nested under migrations/ or app/ would slip through.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
*.env
*.env.*
secrets.env
**/secrets.env
*.pem
*.key

# --- Python build/test residue ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/
# Stale packaging metadata: `pip install -e backend` leaves this behind, and an
# egg-info baked into the image can shadow the metadata the build itself writes.
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
*.log

# Tests and their fixtures are not needed at runtime; excluding them also keeps
# test-only credentials and dumps out of the published image. CI runs pytest
# from a checkout, not from this image.
tests/
scripts/auth_e2e.*
scripts/ingest_e2e.*

# Editor / OS noise
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Docs and local notes — not served by the app. `pyproject.toml` declares
# `readme = "README.md"`, so that one file has to survive for the package build,
# and it has to be anchored: an unanchored `!README.md` re-includes EVERY README
# in the tree, including the ones inside .venv that the rules above excluded.
*.md
!/README.md
